  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThe ridge that started to build overhead on Sunday starts to flatten \nout at the start of the work week, as a deep wave strengthens over \nthe Rockies and guides weak shortwaves through the region. This will \nleave us with upper level zonal flow through Tuesday, with the \naforementioned deep wave forecast to eject off of the Rockies \nWednesday/Thursday. At the start of the week, llvl winds will be out \nof the southwest, resulting in moderate warm advection into the \narea. Mostly dry weather will be seen due to lower moisture, with \nabove normal temperatures favored to continue until at least mid \nweek. As was mentioned above, weak bouts of energy will pass \noverhead ahead of the stronger wave, largely bringing a brief wind \nshift and passing clouds. Monday will be another day of unseasonably \nmild temperatures in the mid 80s for many, with temperatures in the \nlow-mid 70s Tuesday behind a passing system.\n\nTuesday night and through the remainder of the week, an active \npattern will take shape when the deeper wave ejects off of the \nRockies and tracks northeast through the region. Stronger warm \nadvection (35-45+ KT 850 hPa jet) will kick in ahead of this wave, \nfavoring increasing moisture throughout. With plenty of forcing and \nmoisture in place, widespread showers/storms will be seen. The \ntimeframe that guidance is currently highlighting for the more \nwidespread convection will be Wednesday night through Thursday. \nThere remains some uncertainties on further details at this time, \nalong with it being too soon to determine any severe or flash flood \nthreat. Thus, stay tuned for future updates. Temperatures during \nthis timeframe will be a little lower and closer to seasonal \nnormals, as we will be largely blanketed in clouds.\n\nAfter this system passes, guidance is in agreement on a seasonally \nstrong cold front crashing through the region late in the week, \nbringing cooler and dry air.",
